        {
          ":db/id": 17592186106642,
          ":step/content": {
            ":db/id": 17592186107083,
            ":tutorial/content": "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3$ by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 2}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
            ":tutorial/id": "8abc3ed3-0ba8-5dc1-8bf8-437985614b92",
            ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 3273,
            ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
          },
